title: From CNAPP to AI-APP
page: cnapp-to-ai-app.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: AI-APP is a vendor term for a CNAPP that joins code, SOC logs and runtime signals, and developer workstations to one graph, and adds AI agents.

== The 30-second version
layout: text
section: top
A CNAPP maps a cloud estate and finds attack paths. What Wiz (disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz) now calls an AI-APP joins code, logs and runtime signals, and developer workstations to that map, and adds AI agents.
notes:
A CNAPP, a cloud-native application protection platform, maps a cloud estate and finds the combinations of exposure, vulnerability, permission and data that make an attack path.

What Wiz now calls an AI-APP keeps that map and joins three more things to it: the source code and pipelines that build the estate, the logs and runtime signals a SOC investigates, and the developer workstations where code and credentials start. It also treats AI applications as assets to protect. On top sit three AI agents: Red looks for exploitable exposure, Green works out a fix and who owns it, and Blue investigates detections and returns a verdict.

Disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz. The name matters less than the join.

== What CNAPP covered, and where it stopped
layout: list
section: what-cnapp-was
CNAPP's defining idea was a graph of resources, identities, network paths, vulnerabilities and data. That graph described the estate as it stood, and had three blind spots.
item: Where a resource came from
item: What was happening to it
item: Where the credentials started
notes:
Gartner named the CNAPP category in 2021. Its defining idea was context: a graph of resources, identities, network paths, vulnerabilities and data, so that "public, vulnerable, over-privileged and holding customer data" shows up as one problem instead of four tickets.

The three blind spots are the three things the newer platforms join to it. A misconfigured bucket was found in the cloud, but a Terraform file in someone's repository created it, so the fix had to be made in code by someone who never saw the finding. Posture is a snapshot of risk, and whether anyone was exploiting it lived in a different tool, usually a SIEM run by a different team. And cloud intrusions often begin on a developer's machine, as in the CircleCI and LastPass incidents. The laptop belonged to the endpoint team and sat outside the cloud graph entirely.

== Four sources, one graph, three agents
layout: figure
section: what-cnapp-was
figure: aiapp-fig-title
Four sources feed one graph. The agents work from it, and workflows decide which actions run automatically and which wait for a human.
notes:
The figure shows four sources feeding one security graph: code and pipelines, the cloud estate, logs and runtime signals, and developer workstations. The Red, Green and Blue agents work from that graph, and workflows decide which of their actions run automatically and which wait for a human.

The caption says CNAPP was the middle bar plus the cloud box. The three other sources, and the agents above the graph, are what changed. Status is as of October 2026.

== The two meanings of "AI"
layout: compare
section: two-meanings
col: AI as something to protect | Inventories AI applications on cloud AI services, SaaS and custom code | Classifies what each agent's tools can do | Watches model inputs and outputs, tool use and agent identities
col: AI as something that does the work | Red, Green and Blue agents | Use language models to test, fix and investigate | Work from the platform's graph
Wiz announced AI-APP in March 2026. For cloud security teams in general, most of what is new sits in the joins and in the second meaning.
notes:
Wiz announced AI-APP in March 2026 and calls it "the natural evolution" of CNAPP. The "AI" in the name carries two meanings, and it helps to keep them apart.

As something to protect, the platform inventories AI applications built on the managed AI services of AWS, Azure and Google Cloud, on SaaS platforms such as OpenAI and Microsoft Copilot Studio, and in custom code. It classifies what each agent's tools can do, and watches model inputs and outputs, agent tool use and the identities agents act as, for threats such as prompt injection.

As something that does the work, the Red, Green and Blue agents use language models to test, fix and investigate, working from the platform's graph.

For cloud security teams in general, most of what is new sits in the joins and in the second meaning.

== Join one: source code and pipelines
layout: table
section: code
row: Capability | What it does
row: Scanning before production | Dependencies, IaC, secrets and malware, with results in pull requests and the IDE
row: Tracing code to cloud | Links a repository to its image and the running workload
row: Finding the owner | For a leaked secret: commit author, then CODEOWNERS, then maintainers
row: Fixes as pull requests | A suggested change or generated pull request
notes:
The platform connects to version control the way it connects to a cloud account. Repositories are scanned for vulnerable open-source dependencies, infrastructure-as-code misconfigurations, exposed secrets, sensitive data and malware. Since April 2026 the platform also reads GitHub Actions workflow files for excessive permissions and dangerous triggers such as pull_request_target.

Tracing code to cloud means a vulnerability in a running container traces back to the Dockerfile, repository and commit that introduced it, and a code finding can be ranked by whether that code runs somewhere exposed.

Every finding needs a person. For a leaked secret, for example, the platform links the finding to the commit author, falls back to CODEOWNERS files and the mapping of GitHub users, and hands off to the repository's maintainers when the author has left. Ownership is what turns a cloud finding into a ticket or a pull request for a named developer. Because the platform knows which file produced a resource, it can propose the fix where the fix belongs, so the owner reviews a diff, which is how developers already work.

== Join two: logs, runtime and the SOC
layout: compare
section: soc
col: A log-only detection | An access key listed buckets from an unfamiliar address
col: The same detection on the graph | Whose key it is, and what it reaches | Which buckets hold sensitive data | Whether the workload is internet-facing | Which repository deployed it, and its owner
Wiz Defend correlates cloud, SaaS and version-control logs and runtime sensor signals against the graph. The analyst starts with the answers.
notes:
The detection product, Wiz Defend, analyzes cloud and SaaS logs alongside runtime signals from an eBPF-based sensor on VMs, containers and serverless containers. It also ingests the audit logs of supported version control platforms. Detections are correlated against the same graph that holds posture and code.

The correlation is the point. The analyst starts with the answers a senior responder would otherwise spend the first hour assembling.

When the sensor fires on a workload, it can also collect a forensics package: the triggering script or binary, the process tree, shell histories, SSH configuration, the container's drift layer and system logs. Evidence gathered at detection time survives a container that is gone by the time anyone looks.

None of this makes the platform a SIEM replacement by default. Detections also flow out to existing SIEM and SOAR tools.

== Join three: developer workstations
layout: text
section: workstations
The workstation sensor is in private preview for Windows and macOS. It inventories packages, secrets, IDE extensions, AI coding agents and MCP servers.
- Detects known-malicious packages and suspicious behavior
- Can kill the process and rotate or revoke exposed credentials
notes:
Wiz announced the sensor in August 2026, deployed through existing device management. It detects known-malicious packages and suspicious behavior, such as a postinstall script spawning an unexpected process or a secrets scanner reading credential files, and it can kill the process, collect forensic artifacts, and rotate or revoke exposed credentials.

The reason to put a laptop in a cloud graph is reach. A developer's machine holds cloud credentials, package-publishing tokens and access to source code, and AI coding agents now act with those same credentials. The Shai-Hulud npm worm is the case study: its install script ran TruffleHog against the developer's own machine and used what it found to spread.

Private preview means this is a direction, not something to plan around yet.

== The agents: Red, Green and Blue
layout: table
section: agents
row: Component | What it does | Status
row: Red | Tests internet-facing applications and APIs like an attacker | Generally available, July 2026
row: Green | Traces the highest-risk issues to a root cause, proposes fix and owner | Public preview since March 2026
row: Blue | Investigates detections and returns a verdict | Generally available for Wiz Defend customers, March 2026
row: Workflows | Decides when agents run and where a human approves | Generally available, August 2026
Status is as of the page's date, from Wiz's own posts.
notes:
Wiz announced three agents in March 2026, each working from the graph, plus Workflows, a builder that decides when an agent acts on its own and when a human approves.

Red Agent continuously tests internet-facing applications and APIs the way an attacker would, and produces validated findings with proof of execution and reproduction steps. Green Agent takes the highest-risk issues and traces each one to its root cause, then gives a Remediate or Ignore verdict with a confidence score, step-by-step fixes, assignment to the owner it identifies, and a pull request or a hand-off to a coding agent. Blue Agent investigates detections using cloud telemetry, runtime signals, identity context, forensics packages and source-code history.

The status column reflects Wiz's own posts as of the date at the top of the page.

== Blue Agent's five verdicts
layout: text
section: agents
Each verdict carries a confidence level. A verdict is bounded by the evidence behind it.
- Malicious
- Security Test
- Planned Action
- Not Malicious
- Inconclusive
notes:
Two of the five matter most to a SOC. Cloud alerts often turn out to be an authorized test or an engineer making a change, and Security Test and Planned Action give that work names of its own instead of forcing it into false positive.

A verdict is bounded by the evidence behind it. In Wiz's own example, the same detection reads Low Confidence, Inconclusive without a forensics package and High Confidence Malicious with one. Where the sensor is not deployed, the agent reasons over less, and its confidence should drop to match.

Wiz describes routing by confidence: high-confidence containment can run automatically, while lower-confidence decisions go to a person through Slack, Jira, ServiceNow or similar tools. Where to draw that line is the customer's decision, and the page calls it the most consequential setting on the page.

== A worked example
layout: timeline
section: example
event: Workstation | Sensor flags a secrets scan and kills the process
event: Graph | Shows what the exposed token and key reach
event: Version control | Token adds a new GitHub Actions workflow
event: Cloud | Key lists buckets from a new address
event: Blue Agent | One investigation, Malicious, high confidence
event: Workflows | Playbook revokes the token and deactivates the key
An illustration, not a recorded incident. Its first step needs the workstation sensor, in private preview.
notes:
This is an illustration of how the joins are meant to compose, following the shape of the Shai-Hulud worm. It is not a recorded incident, and its first step depends on the workstation sensor, which is in private preview.

A developer installs a package whose postinstall script runs a secrets scanner over the home directory. The sensor flags it, kills the process and records which credentials were on the machine: a GitHub token and an AWS access key. The graph already knows what they reach. The VCS audit log shows the token adding a workflow a few minutes later, and CloudTrail shows the key listing buckets from an address the account has never seen.

Instead of three alerts in three tools, Blue Agent opens with all four signals tied to one developer. Whether the playbook acts automatically or waits for a person is the confidence setting. Green Agent then picks up related posture issues and proposes pull requests.

Each step exists in some separate tool today. The difference is that the endpoint team, the cloud security team, the SOC and the repository owners would each have seen one piece, and connecting them would have depended on someone noticing.

== Not only Wiz
layout: table
section: not-only-wiz
row: Vendor | Starting point | Move
row: Palo Alto Networks | A SOC platform | Cortex Cloud, February 2025
row: CrowdStrike | An endpoint agent | Multi-agent investigations, September 2026
row: Microsoft | Cloud, identity and GitHub | Defender for Cloud with GitHub Code Security, June 2026
row: Wiz | An agentless cloud graph | AI-APP, March 2026
A starting point shapes what is deep and what is thin. Whichever side a platform grew from, test hardest on the side it grew into.
notes:
The same convergence is happening across the market, which is a better reason to understand it than any one product. These three are from the vendors' own announcements.

Palo Alto Networks introduced Cortex Cloud as the next version of Prisma Cloud, merging its CNAPP with cloud detection and response. CrowdStrike correlates application code analysis with cloud entitlements in Falcon Cloud Security, and announced multi-agent investigations that return a verdict with staged response actions. Microsoft made the Defender for Cloud and GitHub Code Security integration generally available and announced discovery of local AI agents and MCP servers on developer machines.

Whichever side a platform grew from, test hardest on the side it grew into.

== What to be skeptical of
layout: list
section: skeptical
The page says none of these are reasons to avoid the approach.
item: The platform becomes a crown jewel
item: A verdict is a probability
item: Ownership is only as good as your metadata
item: Workstation monitoring is employee monitoring
item: Autonomous testing needs scope and permission
item: Consolidation trades seams for concentration
item: The name belongs to a vendor
notes:
These are the costs a launch post leaves out, and most apply to any platform built this way. The next two slides take the first two.

Briefly on the rest: routing a fix to its owner depends on commit history and CODEOWNERS files that reflect reality. A sensor that inventories every package, extension, AI tool and secret on a developer's machine is collecting data about a person's work, so tell developers what it collects and why. An agent that probes internet-facing applications is doing penetration testing, so confirm scope and permission. One platform removes hand-offs, and it also means one vendor's blind spot becomes yours. Ownership of the vendor matters too: Google completed its acquisition of Wiz in March 2026 and said Wiz products would keep working and stay available across major clouds. If you run mainly on AWS or Azure, that commitment is worth tracking over time, as it would be for any security product a cloud provider owns, Microsoft's Defender for Cloud included. Finally, CNAPP came from an analyst firm and AI-APP came from Wiz, while others describe overlapping scope in their own terms. Expect the labels to keep moving, and buy for outcomes rather than acronyms.

== Crown jewel: ask about permissions
layout: text
section: skeptical
One system needs read access to every account, repository and audit log, plus write access for pull requests and containment.
- Can write access be separate and limited?
- Where is code stored, and does it train a model?
notes:
To do what the page describes, one system needs read access to every cloud account, every repository, the audit logs and, with the workstation sensor, every developer laptop. To open pull requests or run containment it also needs write access. That makes the platform one of the most valuable targets in the estate.

Ask which permissions each connector needs, whether write access can be granted separately and limited to specific repositories or actions, where source code is processed and stored and for how long, whether customer code or data trains any model, and how tenants are isolated. Then review those grants as you would any non-human identity with that much reach.

== A verdict is a probability
layout: text
section: skeptical
A false Malicious costs an analyst some time. A confident Not Malicious that closes on its own can cost a breach.
- Keep closure and containment behind human approval until measured
- Sample closed verdicts weekly and read the reasoning
notes:
An agent that sorts detections into five verdicts will sometimes be wrong, and the errors do not cost the same.

Until you have measured how often the agent agrees with your analysts on your own data, keep closure and containment behind human approval, sample closed verdicts every week, and read the reasoning rather than the label. Coverage gaps become confidence gaps, as Wiz's own forensics example shows.

== Questions to ask any vendor
layout: checklist
section: questions
check: Which features are generally available, not preview?
check: What permissions does each connector need?
check: Where is code stored, and does it train a model?
check: Can I see and export the evidence behind each verdict?
check: What can an agent do without human approval?
check: How can I measure agent accuracy on my own data?
check: If we leave, what can we export?
Ask for the answers in a trial on your own environment, not on a slide.
notes:
These apply to Wiz and to every platform that claims to join code, cloud and the SOC. The page lists ten questions, and these are seven of them.

The others cover which logs are ingested, how long they are kept and what is forwarded to your SIEM, how owners are resolved when an owner has left, and for endpoint sensors, what is collected and what the sensor can do on the machine.

Ask for the answers in a trial on your own environment, not on a slide.

== Opinion, labelled as opinion
layout: text
section: authors-note
The page's author, who works at Wiz, thinks the join matters more than any single feature. Whether a product delivers it in your environment is something to test, not take on trust.
notes:
This is the page's author's note, which it labels as opinion from someone who works at Wiz, so weigh it accordingly.

The view is that the join is the significant part. For most of the history of cloud security, the person who found a problem, the person who owned the code behind it and the analyst who would have seen it exploited worked in different tools, often on different teams. One graph across all three, with agents that can reason over it and people who decide what the agents may do alone, is close to what cloud security teams have wanted for a long time.

Whether any product delivers that is something to test, not take on trust, the author included.

== How is AI-APP different from CNAPP?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider what a CNAPP maps, and what it cannot see.
notes:
This is a question from the page's FAQ. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== One graph can connect a finding to its code, owner and exploitation
layout: answer
section: faq
A CNAPP maps the cloud estate. AI-APP, as Wiz uses the term, adds repositories and code owners, logs and runtime detection, and developer workstations (in private preview).
notes:
A CNAPP maps the cloud estate and finds attack paths in it. AI-APP, as Wiz uses the term, adds what the estate came from, what is happening to it, and where credentials start. The practical change is that one graph can connect a finding to the code that produced it, the person who owns it, and any activity exploiting it.

== Does a platform like this replace a SIEM?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider where its detections go, and what else a SIEM holds.
notes:
This is the second FAQ question. Ask it before showing the answer.

== Not by default
layout: answer
section: faq
It analyzes cloud, SaaS and version-control logs against its graph, but detections also flow to existing SIEM and SOAR tools. Retention, non-cloud sources and compliance storage are for you to settle.
notes:
Not by default. The platform analyzes cloud, SaaS and version-control logs against its graph, but detections also flow out to existing SIEM and SOAR tools.

Log retention, non-cloud sources and compliance storage are separate questions to settle for your own environment.
